# Pull Request Rules

When creating or preparing pull requests, follow these conventions for content, pre-PR checks, and commits.

## PR content

- **Title:** Short, imperative. Optionally prefix with type (e.g. `feat: Add Button component`, `fix: Correct modal focus trap`).
- **Description:** Use a simple template:
  - **What** changed (bullet list)
  - **Why** (brief rationale)
  - **How to test** (steps or commands)
  - Optionally link to issue/ticket

```markdown
// BAD: Vague title and empty description
Title: "Updates"
Description: (empty)

// GOOD: Clear title and structured description
Title: feat(button): add disabled state and loading variant
Description:
- What: Added `disabled` and `loading` props to Button; updated styles.
- Why: Needed for form flows and async actions.
- How to test: Run storybook, toggle disabled/loading on Button stories.
- Fixes #123
```

## Pre-PR checklist

Before opening or updating a PR, run:

- `npm run lint` (or `npx eslint .`)
- `npm run format` (or `npx prettier --write .`)
- Tests if present (e.g. `npm test`)

Ensure no new lint or format errors. If the AI introduces them, fix or suggest fixes.

## Commit conventions

Use **Conventional Commits**: `type(scope): short description`.

- **Types:** `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore` (optionally `perf`, `ci`).
- **Scope:** Optional; use lowercase.
- **Subject:** Lowercase, no period at end. One logical change per commit when possible.

```
// BAD
git commit -m "fixed stuff"
git commit -m "WIP"

// GOOD
git commit -m "fix(button): resolve focus trap in modal"
git commit -m "feat: add Card component"
```
